 cables terminate, enabling the connection of various end-user devices. Strategic placement of these points is key to achieving better organization of the home's network infrastructure and facilitates easier network expansion and maintenance [Data: Entities (1173)]. Careful planning regarding the location of each service point is necessary to ensure optimal functionality and integration within the living space [Data: Entities (1173)]. The 'Tomada de Serviço' is described as the physical connector installed at these points [Data: Relationships (1870, 1872)]. Additionally, the proximity of a 'Ponto de Energia Próximo' (nearby power outlet) to the 'Ponto de Serviço Residencial' is considered a necessary requirement for powering active network equipment [Data: Relationships (1875)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Exposed Installation as a Simpler but Aesthetically Inferior Alternative\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Instalação externa/exposta' (exposed installation) method involves installing cables and components visibly, typically secured over surfaces or within apparent raceways. This approach offers significant advantages in terms of ease and speed of installation, straightforward access for maintenance and modifications, and a lower initial cost compared to concealed methods [Data: Entities (1193)]. However, these benefits come at the cost of compromised aesthetics, as cables and components remain visible, detracting from the overall appearance of the space [Data: Entities (1193)]. Furthermore, exposed installations leave cables more vulnerable to physical damage and interference, and may potentially violate safety and aesthetic regulations [Data: Entities (1193)]. This method is also associated with the 'Tomada de Serviço', indicating that exposed outlets and cabling are a recognized, albeit less desirable, installation practice [Data: Relationships (1877)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Necessity of Nearby Power Outlets for Active Network Equipment\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Ponto de Energia Próximo' (nearby power outlet) is an essential element in residential cabling infrastructure, specifically referring to an electrical outlet positioned close to a telecommunications service point. Its primary function is to provide a reliable power source for active network equipment, such as routers, switches, VoIP phones, and cameras, thereby eliminating the need for extension cords and reducing cable clutter [Data: Entities (1191)]. This proximity not only enhances the security and organization of the workspace but is a fundamental requirement for the proper operation of these network devices [Data: Entities (1191)]. The strategic placement of these power outlets requires careful and coordinated planning between electrical and telecommunications teams to meet the specific power demands of the network devices [Data: Entities (1191)]. The relationship between 'Ponto de Energia Próximo' and 'Ponto de Serviço Residencial' underscores the critical need for these power sources to be co-located for functional network setups [Data: Relationships (1875)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"RJ11 Connector for Voice Connectivity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Within the context of residential cabling, the 'RJ11' connector is identified as a specific type of physical connector primarily used for voice connections in 'Tomada de Serviço' (service outlets) [Data: Entities (1203)]. This standardized connector plays a crucial role in establishing reliable voice communication within the home network infrastructure. Its relationship with the 'Tomada de Serviço' indicates that when voice connectivity is a requirement at a service outlet, an RJ11 connector will be employed [Data: Relationships (1885)]. While the broader description of 'Tomada de Serviço' mentions RJ45 for data and coaxial for video, the RJ11 specifically caters to the voice transmission needs.\"\n    